@@ -96,10 +96,24 @@
 task, cgroup, kernfs and worker layout is retained: a task bit uses existing
 space and kernel-private group/worker data is allocated after existing objects.
 
-Memory PSI retains the 4.19 reclaim and compaction annotations, including
-memory.high synchronous reclaim. It does not backport the complete workingset
-page-cache refault and swap-reload classification. Do not interpret zero memory
-pressure as evidence that no file/swap-related stall occurred. Merely marking
-all swap reads as memory pressure would introduce cold-swapin overcounting.
+Memory PSI includes reclaim, compaction, memory.high synchronous reclaim,
+and file-cache workingset refaults. Previously active file pages carry their
+workingset history through LRU deactivation, eviction shadows, eligible refault,
+migration and compound-page splitting. Waiting for those pages to become
+uptodate and submitting their read BIOs are memory stalls. Ordinary cold file
+reads do not become memory stalls just because they perform I/O.
+
+The BCM ABI keeps all previous page/BIO flag numbers unchanged: PG_workingset
+is appended in a free low page-flag bit and BIO_WORKINGSET uses spare bit 12,
+below the existing bit-13 bvec-pool field. No VM-stat enumeration or array is
+grown to add the optional workingset_restore diagnostic counter.
+
+Anonymous/swap workingset detection is not backported in this stage. Filemap
+waits exclude swap-backed pages, and swap_readpage is not indiscriminately
+marked as memory pressure; that would overcount cold swapins on zram/zswap.
+Existing reclaim-time swap stalls still follow the surrounding memstall scope.
+As in the selected upstream BIO annotation, legacy direct I/O using an already
+hot file-backed mapping as its user buffer can inherit the page's workingset
+mark. Ordinary anonymous direct-I/O buffers are not marked by this extension.
 Userspace policy, including systemd-oomd configuration, is separate from these
 kernel interfaces.
